TRIZ, the 39 engineering parameters, the 40 inventive principles and the
classical 39x39 contradiction matrix originate with Genrich Altshuller and
his collaborators (1946-1985), who released the TRIZ methodology into the
public domain by the authors' goodwill. kTRIZ ships the classical parameter
and principle lists and, as of 2026-09-02, a full classical 39x39
contradiction matrix (1248 populated cells).

That matrix is kTRIZ's own reconciled compilation, not a copy of any single
source: it was assembled from four independent, freely available
transcriptions of the classical matrix by a documented majority rule (a cell
is accepted when at least two of the four sources agree on it verbatim), two
of which are themselves MIT-licensed (Antropocosmist/triz-engineering-solver,
sorunokoe/TRIZ-Skills). Full sourcing, the reconciliation method, and known
anomalies are documented in docs/matrix-provenance.adoc. One of the four
sources cited its own provenance as "via a MIT-licensed repository" that, on
verification, in fact carries no licence file at all; kTRIZ does not repeat
that unverified claim and instead treats that source's licensing as unknown,
using it only as one voice among four rather than as a standalone basis for
any cell.

kTRIZ does NOT ship, and will not ship, the Matrix 2003 / Matrix 2010 /
Matrix 2022 data sets (Darrell Mann / Systematic Innovation / CREAX), which
remain under their authors' copyright and are licensed selectively. Holders
of such a licence can supply their own data locally through the pluggable
matrix-source seam: `ContradictionMatrixData.parse` is the parsing entry
point, `ContradictionMatrixSource.fromFile(path)` the hardened file-based
frontend, and the system property `ktriz.matrix.file` swaps
`ContradictionMatrix`'s own process-wide default with no code change. This
seam only accepts files shaped like the classical 39-parameter matrix
(`EngineeringParameter` has exactly 39 members) -- a Matrix 2003/2010/2022
file, which addresses 48 parameters, is structurally rejected, not merely
discouraged.

TRIZ substance-field (Su-Field) analysis -- the six classical field types
(mechanical, thermal, chemical, electric, magnetic, gravitational) and the
five-way judgement of a substance-field triangle -- likewise originates with
Altshuller and is treated here under the same public-domain-by-goodwill
basis as the contradiction matrix. kTRIZ ships only this classical,
six-field taxonomy, never the later MATCEMIB eight-field extension
(Belski/Livotov/Mayer) or any other newer or differently attributed
substance-field framework.

The 5-class structure of Altshuller's 76 TRIZ standard solutions for
Su-Field analysis -- 13 + 23 + 6 + 17 + 17 = 76 -- is sourced from the
MATRIZ wiki's "Standard Inventive Solutions (SIS)" page, (c) MATRIZ, which
states it is "distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons, which
permits use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the
original source is credited" (the page does not name a specific Creative
Commons version). kTRIZ ships only this 5-class structure, not the 76
individual solution texts, which remain a deliberately deferred future item
pending a trustworthy primary source -- see
docs/standard-solutions-provenance.adoc for the full sourcing record and
the sources evaluated and rejected.

Note that "public domain by goodwill" is a community consensus, not a formal
licence grant (there is no CC0 dedication). A concrete digitised
transcription of the matrix may separately attract an EU sui generis
database right even where the underlying facts are free. See kTRIZ-ADR-0003
in the project's design notes; this is an open verification item, not a
settled one.
